WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.839 --> 00:01:24.830 align:start position:0%
 
foreign  foreign  yale has a proud tradition of  involvement in  and ties to military service  many of us have walked by the statue of  nathan hale  who sacrificed his life for his country  in the revolutionary war  we have read the cenotaph on the  quadrangle that honors the hundreds of  yale students and alumni who died  in world war one through our shared  history  the yale community is connected to  thousands of yellies who served in the  first and second world wars  korea and vietnam  this connection extends to the faculty  students  staff and alumni who serve today  the spirit of service and sacrifice that  link our military personnel  is a hallmark of yale's mission  our university is characterized by a  deep commitment  to the common good and to the security  and prosperity of generations to come  in these turbulent times i am more  grateful than  ever for all those who work to  strengthen the fabric of our university  of our country and of communities around  the globe  i appreciate deeply our faculty staff  and alumni  who are veterans yale is committed to  supporting our veterans  through programs like the yale veterans  network  a campus group of students faculty and  staff  and the yale veterans association an  alumni organization  and yale is proud of its students who  currently serve  and the reserved officers training corps  on campus  the coven 19 pandemic has shown us how  necessary it is that two great  institutions of american society  its universities and its professional  armed services  are here to educate future leaders in  how to handle the trials  and uncertainties in life  as we mark veterans day it is vitally  important  for us to remember the human toll of war  individuals have made and continue to  make extraordinary sacrifices in service  to this country  our veterans have my deepest gratitude  and respect  today and every day we thank them  for their service  so  so  so  so  to begin i'd like to thank president  salovey and secretary goff cruz for  leading the university and honoring  veterans each year  i also want to thank dean soti patricia  way and jack beecher  who each play a role in ensuring the  success of student veterans  in 2011 i was looking for some  excitement and a way to pay for college  convinced i knew what i was doing i  enlisted in the marine corps  after working on unmanned aircraft and  serving as a marine security guard  at embassies and consulates i decided to  earn a degree in an officer's commission  through the marine corps enlisted to  officer program  in 2017 after being accepted to yale's  eli whitney students program  i moved to connecticut to begin my  assignment as a full-time undergraduate  after settling in i couldn't wait to  meet my neighbors i was especially  curious about marion  who lives three doors down marianne is  92 years old  and she spends most of her days pulling  weeds and trimming flowers in the garden  before covet 19 she went to the gym  three times a week  when her gym decided to close in march  she was outraged and told me that she  just didn't know how she would stay in  shape  marianne and i don't seem to have much  in common she was born  in 1928 almost 90 years before all  military jobs opened to women  she attended new haven's hill house high  school where she learned how to cook and  sew  despite the many differences in decades  between marian and me  we do have one thing in common her  husband george was a marine  also in new havener george was born in  1923  and was commercial high school's star  basketball player when marion showed me  his team picture  she wanted me to know that despite being  the shortest on the team  he was their best player like many in  his generation  he enlisted soon after graduating in  1941.  george would go on to fight two of the  war's most historic battles  at iwo jima where marines and a navy  corpsman raised the [ __ ] on mount  suribachi  and in okinawa the bloodiest battle of  the pacific war  marian has told me that george never  talked to her about the war  but that he was a marine through and  through that he loved his country  his family and that he was proud to have  served he was outgoing  and in marion's words he had a lot of  parties she told me that if he were  alive today  he would have had some great stories to  tell me but that he never exaggerated  to be fair i don't think there'd be much  room for embellishment  after returning from the war he met  marion at franklin street's falcon dance  hall  on valentine's day 1946 they were  married the next year  settled into civilian life and  eventually moved to the street we live  on today  when george was in his 50s he enrolled  at the university of new haven  and earned a degree in business marianne  was very proud when she told me  that this degree usually took four years  but that george did it in two  i laughed when she said this thinking  about about zach metcalf marine and  timothy dwight  class of 2018 who also did it in two  george and marion were married for 70  years  what i've learned from marion besides  how great life can be at 92  is that the pride that comes with  service is not only felt by us  it's also felt by our families this is  part of the reason we celebrate  veterans day is for those who have  served and it's for marion for people  like my own parents  and for many of you listening today who  admire the service and sacrifice of  someone close to you  i'm reminded of this each time marion  asked me about my experiences in the  marine corps  or invites me inside to show me pictures  of george i'm also reminded of this  when the naval rotc midshipmen and i  gather on the steps of sterling memorial  library  and campus visitors stopped to tell us  about their son or daughter who was  serving  or their own stories from the navy or  the marine corps  i think this message is especially  fitting this year and although we can't  gather together to celebrate  students faculty staff and alumni we're  joined virtually  by more friends and family than probably  ever before  so to all veterans past present and  future thank you for your service and  your sacrifice and happy veterans day  wow  bye  our loving and merciful god of many  names  and all generations we are thankful  beyond measure  for the courage commitment and  sacrifices of our veterans  who have served bravely and selflessly  in the united states  armed forces on land sea or sky  the legacy of their service to our  country and to  our world is one that must never be  forgotten  we remember with deep respect and  unending gratitude  those who have lost their lives in this  service  we are forever in their debt and we  honor their families  whose loss can never really be made  whole  gracious god we also ask for continued  healing  for all those who struggle with the  visible  and invisible scars of war  we ask special regard for those who have  served  but who have not been found and returned  home  and for those who have returned but who  have yet to find themselves  at home we hold close in our hearts  those who are currently deployed and ask  that your gentle hand  watch over them and guide them safely  back  to their loved ones may we never forget  all that our many veterans have done to  protect freedom  in human rights as we work to realize  the kind of world  you our loving god intends for us  may we continue to seek peace peace born  from wisdom and understanding  nurtured with compassion and mercy  strengthened by courage and commitment  for this we say amen  you

